# The root that a WRITE reaches, and the rule it answers to (design.md
# §4.4 the three line shapes, §4.8 `@` parameters, spec:88 *only a declared
# `@` name can be mutated*; panels 010, 015). Two callers in
# `resolve/walk.hero`, and they are the two ways a program writes a value:
# the left of a mutation statement, and an `@` argument at a call site.
#
# **Why this left the walker's knot** (CLAUDE.md §11). `write_root` calls
# nothing in the ring `statement` → `expr` → `block` → `statement`; it is a
# leaf that reads the scope stack and the tables. It sat in `walk.hero` for
# as long as that file stayed under the ceiling, and on 2026-09-03 the
# second caller arrived — `inout_root`, for `@` arguments — and took the
# file past 300. A leaf that leaves a knot costs the reader nothing: the
# map in `walk.hero`'s module doc names this module as the place the write
# rule lives, and the ring is unchanged.
#
# **What `inout_root` closes** (M-robustness-guards step 1). Until that day
# the call site `add(@b, "x")` on a by-value `b` was accepted and corrupted
# the heap — exit 133 or exit 134 with the leak panic, ASan
# heap-use-after-free — because the marker at the call site was checked
# only against the PARAMETER's marker (`check/walk.hero`'s
# `marker_mismatch`), never against the argument's binding. Eight shapes
# were measured before the repair and seven passed `heroes check`: a
# by-value parameter, a `=` local, a field, an index, a nested record, a
# `for` variable, a `match` payload and a generic `@x: T`. One rule closes
# all of them, because every place has exactly one root (§4.10, panel 010):
# the root of an `@` argument is written, so it is held to the rule of the
# left of `@` — a cell or an `@` parameter, never a `=` binding, a loop
# variable, a payload, a top-level declaration or a built-in.

use ast
use diag
use token
use source
use inventory
use resolve/state
use resolve/errors
use resolve/qualified
use resolve/names

# The root of a written place. A write is recorded, and it is NOT a read:
# `v @ 1` alone leaves `v` unused. Writing through a field or an index is a
# write to the root for the same reason — with no aliasing anywhere
# (§4.10), a write nobody reads back is unobservable. Go left this hole
# open (golang/go#20802); Heroes closes it because it has no aliases.
function write_root(@r: state.Resolver, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, id: i64)
    span = a.exprs[id].span
    text = source.span_text(s, span)
    local = state.lookup_local(r, text)

    if !local.is_err()
        at = local.must()

        if !r.out.locals[at].mutable
            state.push_diagnostic(@r, errors.not_mutable(text, r.out.locals[at].kind, span))
            return
        r.out.uses[id] @ .local(at: at)
        r.out.locals[at].writes @ r.out.locals[at].writes + 1
        return

    if !state.top_visible(r, text).is_err()
        state.push_diagnostic(
            @r,
            errors.no_mutable_globals(name: text, what: "top-level declaration", span: span)
        )
        return

    if !inventory.index_of(text).is_err()
        state.push_diagnostic(@r, errors.no_mutable_globals(name: text, what: "built-in", span: span))
        return
    candidates = names.value_candidates(r)
    near = state.near_names(@r, text, candidates)
    state.push_diagnostic(@r, errors.unknown_name(text, near, span))

# The root of an `@` argument, held to the rule of the left of `@`. The
# walker resolved the argument as a READ first (copy in), so an unknown
# name has been reported once already; this adds the write half (copy
# out) and records the write, exactly as `place` does for a statement. The
# parser guarantees the shape — `not_a_place` refuses anything but a name,
# a field path or an index path after `@` — so every arm below that is not
# one of those three is a tree the parser did not produce.
function inout_root(@r: state.Resolver, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, id: i64)
    match a.exprs[id].kind
        .name =>
            match r.out.uses[id]
                .local | .top | .builtin => write_root(@r, a, s, id)
                .module | .unresolved    => return
        .field f =>
            # `f(@geom.ORIGIN)`: a qualified constant is a top-level
            # declaration, and `place` says the same for the statement.
            if !qualified.module_at(r, a, s, f.base).is_err()
                state.push_diagnostic(
                    @r,
                    errors.no_mutable_globals(name: source.span_text(s, f.name), what: "top-level declaration", span: f.name)
                )
                return
            inout_root(@r, a, s, f.base)
        .index i => inout_root(@r, a, s, i.base)

        .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .char_lit | .bool_lit | .null_ptr => return
        .hole | .unary | .binary | .call | .method | .variant_case           => return
        .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error          => return

## Two `@` arguments of one call

# **Panel 010's alias refusal, ratified 2026-08-04 and missing from the
# compiler until 2026-09-04** (defect 011). `shift(a: @n, b: @n)` copies
# `n` in twice and copies it out twice, so the call's answer depends on
# which copy-out lands last: `10` under left-to-right, `11` under the
# reference reading every mainstream language installs. Nothing specifies
# the order, so the form is refused — and the refusal is the precondition
# that makes spec:80's *"No aliasing exists anywhere"* true, because two
# copy-outs landing on one place is aliasing of the destination.
#
# **PLACES, not roots, and the difference is two working programs**
# (panel 110). design.md:1298 says *"comparing roots is a complete alias
# test"*, and it over-shoots: `shift(a: @p.x, b: @p.y)` and
# `shift(a: @xs[0], b: @xs[1])` reach disjoint memory, print the right
# answers today, and are clean under `--sanitize` even with the record and
# the array SHARED — measured, both shapes, before this was written. The
# unshare is why: the first one takes the refcount to 1, so the second
# moves nothing. §12 holds a refusal to a feature's standard, and those
# two are the programs a root test would have made wrong. So the walk goes
# step by step and stops at the first pair that is DECIDABLY different: two
# field names, or two indices written as plain decimal digits. Everything
# else — a variable index, a spelling this pass does not decode, one path
# being a prefix of the other — stays overlapping, which is the loud
# direction (CLAUDE.md §11). `f(a: @xs[i], b: @xs[j])` is still refused,
# which is exactly the over-rejection design.md:1299 authorises.
#
# **What is NOT this rule, measured before it was written**: an `@`
# argument nested inside another call's argument list — `outer(@n, k:
# bump(@n))` — keeps both mutations. Three shapes were run and all three
# printed the same answer, because a call's copy-in happens after its
# arguments are evaluated. Two copy-outs of ONE call are the ambiguity;
# two calls in sequence are ordered.
#
# **Why nobody noticed for a month**, and it is two accidents rather than
# one. design.md's three examples are written `shift(a @ n, b @ n)`, a
# spelling `misplaced_mutable_marker` refuses today, so anybody who pasted
# one read that diagnostic as the rule firing. And the unlabelled
# `shift(@n, @n)` fires `needs_label` first, so the alias question is
# reached only once both labels are written — the one form nothing tested.

# The step this pass cannot decide. Two of them never prove two places
# apart, not even against each other: `xs[i]` and `xs[j]` may be one
# element, and `ast.hero:109` keeps a literal's TEXT rather than its value
# — decoding is the checker's job — so `xs[0x0]` is a spelling this pass
# declines to guess at rather than one it reads.
constant UNDECIDED_STEP: str
    "[?]"

# A place as steps from its root, root first: `#3`, `.x`, `[0]`. An error
# means the root is not a local — a top-level declaration, a built-in, a
# module, or a name with no answer — and `inout_root` above has already
# refused every one of those, so this rule has nothing left to say about
# it. Every step asks the VALUE in hand (CLAUDE.md §11): what the resolver
# recorded for this expression, never a premise about what the walk did
# first.
function place_path(r: state.Resolver, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, id: i64) -> [str]?
    match a.exprs[id].kind
        .name =>
            match r.out.uses[id]
                .local l => return ok(["#" + l.at.to_str()])
                .top | .builtin | .module | .unresolved => return fail(
                    "not_a_local",
                    "the root of this place is not a local"
                )
        .field f =>
            base = place_path(r, a, s, f.base)

            if base.is_err()
                return base

            return ok(base.must().push("." + source.span_text(s, f.name)))
        .index i =>
            base = place_path(r, a, s, i.base)

            if base.is_err()
                return base

            return ok(base.must().push(index_step(a, s, i.index)))

        .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .char_lit | .bool_lit | .null_ptr => return fail(
            "not_a_place",
            "the parser refuses this after `@`"
        )
        .hole | .unary | .binary | .call | .method | .variant_case => return fail(
            "not_a_place",
            "the parser refuses this after `@`"
        )
        .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return fail(
            "not_a_place",
            "the parser refuses this after `@`"
        )

# One index, as a step. Only plain decimal digits name an element this
# pass may compare: `0` does, `0x0` and `i` do not.
function index_step(a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, id: i64) -> str
    match a.exprs[id].kind
        .int_lit =>
            text = source.span_text(s, a.exprs[id].span)

            if plain_decimal(text)
                return "[" + text + "]"

            return UNDECIDED_STEP

        .name | .field | .index | .float_lit | .str_lit | .char_lit => return UNDECIDED_STEP
        .bool_lit | .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .call    => return UNDECIDED_STEP
        .method | .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr         => return UNDECIDED_STEP
        .if_expr | .match_expr | .error                             => return UNDECIDED_STEP

# Digits and nothing else, so the text IS the value. `_` between digits,
# a base prefix and a sign all say no: this pass compares spellings and
# only a spelling that can mean one number is safe to compare.
function plain_decimal(text: str) -> bool
    if text.len() == 0
        return false
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] < '0' || text[i] > '9'
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# Whether two places may reach one value. False only where a step proves
# them apart; a prefix (`@p` against `@p.x`) is an overlap, and so is any
# pair whose difference is undecided.
function overlaps(x: [str], y: [str]) -> bool
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < x.len() && i < y.len()
        if x[i] != y[i]
            return x[i] == UNDECIDED_STEP || y[i] == UNDECIDED_STEP
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# The label an argument arrived under, so the second one can name the
# first: `shift(a: @n, b: @n)` says *this call's `a` argument*.
function label_of(s: source.Source, arg: ast.Arg) -> str?
    if arg.name.is_err()
        return fail("unlabelled", "this argument carries no label")

    return ok(source.span_text(s, arg.name.must()))

# Every `@` argument of one call against every earlier one. Called ONCE,
# after the walk over the arguments, because it compares the places that
# walk resolved.
function refuse_aliased_places(@r: state.Resolver, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, given: [ast.Arg])
    paths: [[str]] @ []
    labels: [str?] @ []

    for arg in given
        if !arg.mutable
            continue
        path = place_path(r, a, s, arg.value)

        if path.is_err()
            continue
        clash: i64? @ fail("none", "no earlier `@` argument of this call overlaps this one")
        i: i64 @ 0

        while i < paths.len()
            if clash.is_err() && overlaps(x: paths[i], y: path.must())
                clash @ ok(i)
            i @ i + 1

        if clash.is_err()
            paths @ paths.push(path.must())
            labels @ labels.push(label_of(s, arg))
            continue
        state.push_diagnostic(
            @r,
            aliased_mutable_arguments(name: source.span_text(s, a.exprs[arg.value].span), first: labels[clash.must()], span: a.exprs[arg.value].span)
        )

# The caret is on the SECOND argument, because the first one is fine and
# the second is the one to change.
#
# **The repair is a `guess`, and the reason is the rule's own** (panel
# 110). A fix that passed a copy would have to CHOOSE which of the two
# answers the author meant, and that divergence is the whole reason the
# form is refused. It is not a theory: `n: i64 @ 0` / `t: i64 @ n` /
# `shift(a: @n, b: @t)` was built and run, and it prints **1** where the
# program prints 10 and the reference reading gives 11 — a THIRD answer.
# CLAUDE.md §9's harness asks an applied `certain` fix to compile, not to
# mean the same thing, so shipping this one `certain` would let `--apply`
# turn a 10-program into a 1-program with CI green. `d.fixes` is a list of
# ALTERNATIVES in this tree (`number.hero:105-108` ships two `guess`
# spellings of one literal), and two readings make two guesses.
function aliased_mutable_arguments(name: str, first: str?, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    other: str @ "an earlier `@` argument of this call"

    if !first.is_err()
        other @ "this call's `" + first.must() + "` argument"
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "aliased_mutable_arguments",
        message: "`" + name + "` overlaps " + other + " — two `@` arguments may not reach one value: each of them copies out, and nothing says which copy-out lands last",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "with two copies of one place landing back on it the call's answer would depend on the copy-out order, and that is what `no aliasing exists anywhere` rules out (panel 010)"
    )
    d.fixes @ d.fixes.push(
        diag.Fix(title: "pass a copy for one of them: declare a second cell holding the same value, pass `@` that cell, and write it back after the call", replacement: "", span: span, certainty: .guess)
    )
    d.fixes @ d.fixes.push(
        diag.Fix(title: "or make the two places different: distinct fields (`@p.x` and `@p.y`) and distinct literal indices (`@xs[0]` and `@xs[1]`) are accepted", replacement: "", span: span, certainty: .guess)
    )
    return d
